/tmp/paper2505.00298.atjZCp/src/main.tex:616:\subsection{Hardness of approximating IDPSTP}
/tmp/paper2505.00298.atjZCp/src/main.tex:617:In this subsection, we show that, unless $P=NP$, any approximation algorithm for the IDPSTP problem has a guarantee of $\Omega(n^{{1/3}-\epsilon})$. This result is derived by constructing a reduction from the Directed 2-Linkage problem, where the reduction is inspired by that used in \cite{Che-Sal} for the internally-disjoint directed Steiner tree problem. %Before giving the detailed proof, we need to introduce a concise notation for directed paths. For a directed path $L$ that consists of arcs $x_{1}x_{2},x_{2}x_{3},\cdots,x_{k-1}x_{k}$, we denote $L$ by its full vertex sequence in traversal order: $L=x_{1}-x_{2}-\cdots-x_{k}$. For example, a directed path $L$ consisting of arcs $u_{1}u_{2},u_{2}u_{3},u_{3}u_{4}$ is denoted as $L=u_{1}-u_{2}-u_{3}-u_{4}$.
/tmp/paper2505.00298.atjZCp/src/main.tex:620:    For any given instance of IDPSTP, it is NP-hard to approximate the solution within $O(n^{{1/3}-\epsilon})$ for any $\epsilon> 0$.
