    %If we remove all the $T_{i}'$ containing a vertex from $U$ when we are left with at least two trees. Without loss of generality assume that $T_{1}'$ and $T_{2}'$ do not contain any vertex from $U$. Let $P'$ and $Q'$ are all vertices of $T_{1}'\backslash S$ and $T_{2}'\backslash S$, respectively. We can claim that each edge $e\in E(H)$ has a vertex from $P'$ and a vertex from $Q'$, since for each edge $e\in E(H)\subseteq S$, it has an arc into it in $T_{1}'$ and an arc into it in $T_{2}'$. Finally, we can color the vertices in $P'$ and $Q'$ red and blue respectively, and for those vertices in $V(D)\backslash (P^{'}\cup Q')$, randomly assign either red or blue. This shows that $H$ is 2-colorable, and completes the proof.
