%The concept of pendant-tree connectivity was also introduced by Hager \cite{Hager} in 1985, which is specialization of generalized connectivity (or $k$-tree-connectivity) but a generalization of classical connectivity. For an $S$-tree, if the degree of each vertex in $S$ is equal to one, then this tree is called a pendant $S$-Steiner tree. Two pendant $S$-Steiner trees $T_{1}$ and $T_{2}$ are said to be internally disjoint if $E(T_{1})\cap E(T_{2})=\varnothing$ and $V(T_{1})\cap V(T_{2})=S$. For $S\subseteq V(G)$ and $|S|\geq 2$, we use $\tau_{S}(G)$ to denote the maximum number of internally disjoint pendant $S$-Steiner trees in $G$, which is called the local pendant-tree connectivity. For an integer $k$ with $2\leq k\leq n$, pendant-tree $k$-connectivity is defined as
