Conversely, assume that $\tau_{S,r}(D)\geq \ell$ and let $\{T'_i\mid i\in [\ell]\}$ be a set of $\ell$ pairwise internally-disjoint pendant $(S,r)$-trees in $D$. As $|U| = \ell-2$, there are at least two of these trees, say $T_1'$ and $T_2'$, which contain no vertex from $U$. Let $R'$ (resp. $B'$) be all in-neighbours of $E(H)$ in $T_1'$ (resp. $T_2'$), that is, $R' = N_{T_1'}^-(E(H))$ and $B' = N_{T_2'}^-(E(H))$ (moreover, $R'\cap B'=\emptyset$, as $T_1'$ and $T_2'$ are internally-disjoint). Since each $e\in E(H)$ has an in-arc in $T'_1$  (resp. $T'_2$), $e$ must contain at least a vertex from $R'$ (resp. $B'$). Finally, any vertex in $V(H)\setminus (R'\cup B')$ can be assigned randomly to either $R'$ or $B'$. In this way, we get a proper $2$-coloring of $H$, that is, $H$ is $2$-colorable. This completes the proof.

