@@ -1,210 +0,0 @@
-# arXiv 2505.00298 — internally-disjoint pendant Steiner trees
-
-**Status: locally verified, but not yet shippable.** Every module gate passes at
-the candidate shipping preset. The required four-vendor oracle run could not
-make a scoreable call because the configured OpenRouter key returned HTTP 403
-`Key limit exceeded` on every redraw. The script-authored transcripts are kept;
-service errors are not counted as solver failures, so no oracle hardness verdict
-is claimed.
-
-| profile field | value |
-|---|---|
-| Track | A — structural hardness |
-| native domain | combinatorics |
-| object regime | finite discrete |
-| computational core | CSP/SAT |
-| certificate | exact symbolic two-set partition |
-| intuition | constraint propagation through not-all-equal triples |
-| domain essentiality | licensed reduction |
-| reduction | paper-licensed: Section 2.2, Theorem 2.6 |
-
-## Problem and construction
-
-Yu and Sun's [*Internally-disjoint Pendant Steiner Trees in
-Digraphs*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.00298) asks for arc-disjoint out-trees
-which share only a prescribed terminal set and in which every terminal has
-degree one. Section 1.2 supplies those exact definitions. This family uses the
-paper's own Theorem 2.6 reduction, not an external graph encoding.
-
-The solver is handed a 3-uniform hypergraph and the symmetric digraph defined
-by the theorem: a root, one vertex per hypergraph vertex, one terminal per
-hyperedge, a clique on the variable vertices, root-to-variable adjacencies, and
-incidence adjacencies. The requested certificate is a red/blue partition in
-which every hyperedge contains both colours. The theorem's proof expands each
-class deterministically into one pendant out-tree. `verify` performs that
-expansion and checks exact arcs, indegrees, reachability, terminal degrees,
-arc-disjointness, and the common vertex set. It never reads the planted answer.
-
-Generation is inverse: first sample a balanced colouring, then sample a simple,
-connected, degree-regular hypergraph around it. Every variable has degree six,
-and one-red and two-red triples occur equally often. Thus the witness exists
-before the instance does, without solving the generated CSP.
-
-## Why Track A
-
-Theorem 2.6 proves NP-completeness for symmetric digraphs when
-`ell = 2` is fixed and `k = |S|` is part of the input—the exact regime here,
-because there is one terminal per hyperedge. The essential easy-regime warning
-is Theorem 2.3: if both `k` and `ell` are fixed, the symmetric case is polynomial
-by skeleton enumeration and fixed disjoint-paths tests. This generator makes
-`k = 2n + 1` grow at degree six, so it does not fall into that case. Theorem 2.4
-also confirms that fixed `k` becomes hard only when `ell` is input; that is a
-different regime from this family.
-
-The theorem is worst-case and does **not** prove this planted distribution hard.
-The empirical basis is the construction-aware panel. At `n=198`, NAE-DPLL with
-forced-third-vertex propagation reached its 4,001st node after 4,521,833 exact
-edge inspections and 80,893 propagations without finding a witness (1.494 s in
-the recorded shipping measurement). It failed 0/8 audit seeds, as did local
-co-occurrence outliers, left-to-right greedy colouring, 256 min-conflicts
-restarts, and a signed pair-co-occurrence spectral relaxation. A discarded
-prototype at `n=150` was not good enough: DPLL and min-conflicts each solved
-2/8 seeds, which is why the released ladder starts at 198.
-
-## Worked demo (`seed=0`)
-
-The complete rendered demo is:
-
-```text
-TWO INTERNALLY-DISJOINT PENDANT STEINER OUT-TREES
-
-A directed out-tree rooted at r is a directed tree in which r has
-indegree 0 and every other vertex has indegree 1.  The total degree of
-a vertex is its indegree plus outdegree.  For a terminal set S containing
-r, a pendant (S,r)-tree is an out-tree rooted at r that contains every
-vertex of S and gives every vertex of S total degree exactly 1.  Two such
-trees are internally-disjoint when they have no common arc and their
-common vertex set is exactly S.
-
-The symmetric digraph D has root r, variable vertices v1,...,v6,
-and edge-terminal vertices e1,...,e4.  'Symmetric' means every
-listed adjacency supplies both directed arcs.  The following rules are
-the complete definition of D; there are no other arcs:
-1. r is adjacent to every variable vertex v1,...,v6.
-2. Every two distinct variable vertices are adjacent.
-3. Variable vi is adjacent to terminal ej exactly when vi belongs to
-   hyperedge ej in the list below.
-4. No two edge-terminals are adjacent, and r is adjacent to no edge-terminal.
-
-The terminal set is S={r,e1,...,e4}; r is the root.
-The target is a packing of exactly two internally-disjoint pendant
-(S,r)-trees.
-
-Use the following compact certificate from the construction of these
-trees.  Partition the 1-based variable indices into nonempty sets red
-and blue so that every displayed hyperedge contains at least one index
-of each colour.  The checker expands each colour class C into one tree:
-it uses the smallest index of C as its anchor, includes r->anchor and
-anchor->vi for every other vi in C, and for every ej includes vi->ej
-from the smallest-indexed member of C that lies in ej.  It then checks
-the resulting directed trees against D and the definition above.
-
-Hyperedges (the order of vertices within an edge is irrelevant):
-e1: v4 v5 v6
-e2: v2 v6 v3
-e3: v4 v2 v1
-e4: v3 v5 v1
-
-Return each integer 1,...,6 exactly once across the two lists.
-List order is irrelevant, repeats are forbidden, and exchanging the
-names red and blue is allowed.  Indices are 1-based and bounds are inclusive.
-Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags, as one JSON object
-with exactly the keys "red" and "blue" and integer-array values.
-Example syntax: <answer>{"red":[1,3],"blue":[2,4]}</answer>
-Output nothing else inside the tags.
-```
-
-One answer is `{"blue":[2,3,5],"red":[1,4,6]}`.
-`verify(inst, inst["answer"])` returns `(True, "ok")`. Adding vertex 1
-to `blue` while leaving it in `red` returns
-`(False, "red and blue overlap at a vertex")`. This six-vertex instance
-is hand-solvable by testing the 31 partitions with vertex 1 fixed red; in
-fact, 13 are valid.
-
-## Difficulty presets
-
-| preset | hypergraph vertices | degree | hyperedges / edge-terminals | digraph vertices | status |
-|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
-| demo | 6 | 2 | 4 | 11 | hand-scale |
-| easy | 198 | 6 | 396 | 595 | candidate shipping preset; all local gates pass |
-| medium | 222 | 6 | 444 | 667 | reserve escalation |
-| hard | 246 | 6 | 492 | 739 | final writable rung |
-
-`SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY` is `easy`. It is a candidate designation only until a
-scoreable bare oracle run returns `hardened`. If all named rungs are solved,
-`escalate` has one last fixed-degree rung at `n=256`; only beyond that does the
-256-atom answer cap force `cap_bound`.
-
-## Gate results
-
-| gate | result | measurement |
-|---|---|---|
-| G1 | pass | 12/12 preset/seed certificates verified and JSON-round-tripped |
-| G2 | pass | 5/5 corruptions rejected with five distinct reasons |
-| G3 | pass | tagged JSON recovered through prose and a Markdown fence |
-| G4 | pass | 0/200,000 structure-aware guesses; candidate space `2^197-1` |
-| G5 | pass | shipping density 0/200,000; DPLL 4,001 nodes, 4,521,833 inspections, 1.494 s; demo exact count 13/31 |
-| G6 | pass | five attacks, each 0/8; DPLL is the domain-standard attack |
-| G7 | pass | doubled from 198 to 396 vertices; planted certificate still verifies |
-| G8 | pass | 40/40 composed invariance/witness checks; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
-| G9(c) | pass | 702 characters, about 176 tokens, 198 atoms, 198 intended colour assignments |
-
-The G4 sampler is uniform over every nonempty two-partition after removing
-global colour exchange; it already enforces shape, coverage, uniqueness, and
-nonempty parts. It does not condition on balanced class sizes, because balance
-is not promised in the statement.
-
-## Oracle loop and G9 diagnostics
-
-No row below is a solver failure. All are service errors and consumed no
-attempt. The main transcript is the bare, no-hint evidence that would ship.
-
-| arm | model draws | seeds | scoreable solved / attempts | outcome |
-|---|---|---|---:|---|
-| bare | Gemini, Claude, Claude, Claude | 1422300321, 907616750, 688473513, 137363164 | unavailable / 0 | four HTTP 403 errors |
-| structural | Terra, Gemini, Terra, Terra | 1002717238, 2040339717, 1131060283, 213067794 | unavailable / 0 | four HTTP 403 errors |
-| placebo | Terra, Gemini, Terra, Gemini | 1944369424, 1669675492, 1047366748, 1112328566 | unavailable / 0 | four HTTP 403 errors |
-
-`hinted - placebo` and the hint verdict are unavailable. Consequently, there is
-no evidence yet about whether forced-colour propagation helps the model. The
-size figures remain 702 characters, approximately 176 tokens, 198 atomic
-entries, and 198 intended post-insight colour assignments.
-
-## Use
-
-```python
-import gen_2505_00298 as g
-
-inst = g.make_instance(seed=0, **g.DIFFICULTY["demo"])
-question = g.render(inst)
-answer = g.parse_answer(
-    'work... <answer>{"red":[1,4,6],"blue":[2,3,5]}</answer>'
-)
-assert g.verify(inst, answer) == (True, "ok")
-```
-
-After restoring a usable OpenRouter key, rerun the bare loop and both G9 arms.
-Once the bare verdict is `hardened`, emit from the repository root with:
-
-```bash
-bash scripts/emit.sh 2505.00298 20 easy
-```
-
-## Caveats
-
-- NP-completeness is worst-case; the attack panel is finite evidence, not a
-  distributional theorem. A better planted-NAE algorithm could invalidate the
-  Track A claim.
-- The exact DPLL attack is capped at 4,000 nodes. No industrial SAT/SMT or ILP
-  solver was available under the dependency rule. The spectral test is a basic
-  pair relaxation, not an SDP, and min-conflicts uses a fixed restart budget.
-- The rooted ten-round Weisfeiler–Leman deck is invariant under vertex and
-  hyperedge relabelling but is not a complete hypergraph isomorphism canonizer;
-  exotic non-isomorphic collisions can occur.
-- A uniform random-partition density is not a language-model posterior. It says
-  only that blind, correctly shaped guesses are rare under the declared prior.
-- The digraph is specified by exact construction rules rather than listing its
-  dense variable clique arc by arc. `verify` nevertheless expands and checks the
-  two native out-trees against those rules.
-- The oracle pool remains untested because of the exhausted OpenRouter key. This
-  is the outstanding blocker to a genuine shipping claim.
